  actor: jgstern_agent  # d5f8
  clock: 1  # d5f8
  nonce: d5f8  # d5f8
  data:  # d5f8
    kind: invariant  # d5f8
    title: "python: bare import of a same-directory module (tests/test_a.py: import conftest) stays unresolved and reads as third-party"  # d5f8
    status: violated  # d5f8
    priority: 3  # d5f8
    parent:  # d5f8
    tags:  # d5f8
    - analyzers  # d5f8
    - python  # d5f8
    - import_resolution  # d5f8
    isbefore: []  # d5f8
    duplicate_of: []  # d5f8
    not_duplicate_of: []  # d5f8
    pr_ref:  # d5f8
    description: "Found while fixing INV-nuraf-nuvif-pifut-budoh-gafim-midab-juvuv-zonin (dependency linker counted first-party imports).\n\nRepro: repo with src/pkg/{__init__,a,b}.py, tests/test_a.py containing `import conftest` and tests/conftest.py, pyproject.toml with no dependencies. The Python analyzer resolves `from pkg.a import fa` (src-layout) to `python:src/pkg/a.py:...`, but `import conftest` from tests/test_a.py stays unresolved as `python:conftest:0-0:module:module` (dst_ref ExternalRef(module_path='conftest')) although tests/conftest.py is analyzed. Python puts the importing script's directory on sys.path (and pytest's rootdir insertion does the same for test dirs), so a bare import of a same-directory module names first-party code.\n\nConsequence: the edge reaches the graph as an external boundary, and `hypergumbo survey` on that repo still prints 'DEPENDENCY-LINKER linker found 1 third-party Rust/Python import edges but 0 TOML dependency declarations' after the INV-nuraf fix (which keys on resolution: dst is an in-repo symbol id). Every other consumer of resolution (supply-chain ecosystem/directness stamps, io-boundaries, verify-claims disclosure) sees a phantom third-party module too.\n\nFix direction: Python import resolution falls back to a module in the importing file's own directory for a bare absolute import that matches no other in-repo module. Not done in the INV-nuraf PR because the dependency linker should read the resolution verdict rather than re-derive it by name."  # d5f8
    fields:  # d5f8
      statement: "A Python import that names a module analyzed in the importing file's own directory resolves to that module's in-repo file id."  # d5f8
      root_cause: "Not investigated: the Python import resolver does not try the importing file's own directory for a bare absolute import (observed, resolver code not yet read)."  # d5f8
- op: discuss  # dd6d
  at: '2026-10-29T07:46:16Z'  # dd6d
  by: agent  # dd6d
  actor: jgstern_agent  # dd6d
  clock: 2  # dd6d
  nonce: dd6d  # dd6d
  message: "PREMISE CHECK 2026-10-29 (dev 4892851370). VERDICT: CURRENT, WIDER than the title, narrower in consequence. Fresh fixture (src/pkg/{__init__,a,b}.py, tests/{conftest,test_a}.py, scripts/{util,run}.py, pyproject with no deps), `hypergumbo survey`: tests/test_a.py `import conftest` -> python:conftest:0-0:conftest:external_symbol, AND scripts/run.py `import util` -> python:util:0-0:util:external_symbol -- not a test-directory case; any same-directory bare import. `from pkg.a import fa` still resolves (src-layout). The DEPENDENCY linker line now reads 'found 3 third-party Rust/Python import edges' (conftest, util, and the genuinely third-party pytest). STALE TEXT: the dst id is now ...:conftest:external_symbol, not ...:module:module. NARROWER: CALLS through those modules already resolve in-tree (test_a -> tests/conftest.py:helper, run.main -> scripts/util.py:util, both 0.85), so only the IMPORT edge and its consumers see the phantom module. ROOT CAUSE, now read: _extract_import_edges (py.py:2657) looks both arms up in module_to_file_id by the import's dotted name only (`import X` -> module_to_file_id.get(X); `from X import Y` -> .get(X.Y) or .get(X)); the map is keyed on repo-root/src-layout dotted names (tests.conftest, scripts.util), and there is no fallback to the importing file's own directory. FAMILY: that code's docstring is supply:F4 (INV-nuzas) -- the same first-party-reads-as-external family; surfaced to the owner as a possible child of INV-nuzas, not folded."  # dd6d
